<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=personal-computer">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Personal computer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PC, home computer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A personal computer is a computer designed to be operated directly by one user, rather than shared by many through time-sharing like a minicomputer or mainframe. It was made possible by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/integrated-circuit">integrated circuit</a> and the microprocessor, the first single-chip one being the Intel 4004 of 1971; the Micral N, built from 1972, was the earliest commercial, non-kit microcomputer based on a microprocessor. The Altair 8800 of 1974 was the first commercially successful personal computer, the Commodore PET, Apple II and TRS-80 brought ready-assembled machines to a mass market in 1977, and the IBM Personal Computer of 1981 set the standard its compatibles followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/personal-computer.jpg" alt="Personal computer" width="330" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IBM_PC_5150.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their company went on to dominate <b>personal computer</b> software.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Start menu and taskbar set the pattern of the <b>personal computer</b> desktop for decades.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Apple unveiled the Lisa, a <b>personal computer</b> with a graphical interface and a mouse.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Apple's Macintosh brought the mouse and graphical interface to an affordable <b>personal computer</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1972</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Which machine was the first personal computer is disputed: PC Magazine called IBM's SCAMP prototype of 1973 the world's first personal computer, the MCM/70 of 1973 is described as the first personal desktop microcomputer, and many consider the Altair 8800 of 1974 the first true one.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">Transistor<span>1947</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/integrated-circuit">Integrated circuit<span>1958</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=personal-computer">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
